{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfggWH41fgXcBthS2YAv8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the 8 test statistics to `iter_3/critique/test_stats/`. Each one returns a finite number on the observed data in 30 ms or less, well within the 5 s limit per call. I didn't run the posterior-predictive check; the pipeline runs that next.\n\nWhat each one tests, and what the incumbent model can't express:\n\n| Statistic | Observed | What it tests |\n|---|---|---|\n| `cross_cue_consistency_corr` | 0.115 | Whether people who agree with the majority on switch-driven pairs also agree on pairs where switching is equal. A high value would point to one overall noise trait per person; the model gives each cue its own independent person-level weight. |\n| `anti_alternator_share` | 0.067 | Share of people who pick the higher-switch sequence on fewer than 40% of pairs that differ in switching. This tests the shape of the new population of personal ideal switching rates. |\n| `participant_left_rate_sd` | 0.072 | How much people differ in their left/right lean, a check on the model's side-bias spread. |\n| `model_blind_pair_polarization` | 0.0120 | How lopsided choices are on pairs the model treats as identical (same span, switch count, chunk variety and rule-built status). Strong preferences there would mean a missing cue. |\n| `switch_vs_balance_conflict` | 0.677 | Pairs where the sequence with more switches also has a worse heads/tails balance: which cue wins? |\n| `run_length_variety_equal_switch` | 0.518 | With switch counts equal, do people prefer sequences with a mix of run lengths? The model doesn't see run lengths. |\n| `ending_alternation_equal_switch` | 0.521 | With switch counts equal, do people prefer a sequence that ends on a switch? The model ignores where switches happen. |\n| `interior_longest_run_choice` | 0.383 | With switch counts and longest run equal, do people prefer the sequence whose longest streak is in the middle rather than at an end? The model ignores where streaks fall. |\n\nI avoided repeating ideas from the three earlier rounds.\n\nThe shared filesystem was very slow, and `/tmp` sits on it, so the bash heredoc method kept hanging. I stopped the stuck background write and wrote the last four files with `printf` instead. Because of that, the side-bias statistic's file is named `participant_left_rate_sd.py` rather than `side_bias_sd.py`. I checked that the folder holds exactly these 8 files, with no leftover from the stopped write."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":701,"cache_read_input_tokens":71728,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":701,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"09df3ab0-d8e2-4a0e-a71f-6d812a7984ef","uuid":"22bafd8d-fe66-4a15-bd02-7c8946610f70","timestamp":"2026-10-04T05:56:47.789Z","request_id":"req_011CfggWGY1kNDgcoY7U8NBT"}
